Feature: Mutation concurrency is resolved from the launching host
  Every Stryker mutation entry point sizes its worker count from the host it is
  launched on, instead of reading a constant frozen into a config file. The pure
  calculation already exists (recommendMutationConcurrency, BL-427) and is not
  changed here; what these scenarios pin down is the missing caller — something
  each entry point goes through before Stryker starts, which reads the host,
  computes the number, passes it to Stryker, and says out loud what it chose.

  DRAFT — not executable yet. This file is a .draft on purpose: no step handlers
  exist for these scenarios, and a live .feature would make the acceptance runner
  hard-fail repo-wide (BL-233). Materialize it to
  specs/features/BL-786-mutation-concurrency-host-resolved.feature in the same
  parcel as its step handlers, and point the ticket's acceptance: at it then.

  Entry points are enumerated from extension/package.json at implementation time.
  As of 2026-08-23 there are TWO — mutation and mutation:lets-talk-cursor-bridge.
  There is no mutation:lets-talk-core script; stryker.letsTalkCore.config.json is
  an orphan config with no invoker. If a script for it is added, scenario 03's
  Examples table and scenario 04 must both grow to cover it.

  Background:
    Given the declared per-worker peak RSS is 783 MB (821121024 bytes)
    And the declared RAM reserve is 2048 MB

  # BL-786 mutation-concurrency-host-resolved-01
  Scenario Outline: The resolved concurrency is sized from the host's readings
    Given a host with <cores> cores and <free_ram_mb> MB of free RAM
    When the "mutation" entry point resolves its concurrency
    Then the resolved concurrency is <resolved>

    Examples:
      | cores | free_ram_mb | resolved |
      | 20    | 10282       | 10       |
      | 20    | 1024        | 1        |
      | 4     | 32768       | 4        |

  # BL-786 mutation-concurrency-host-resolved-02
  Scenario: The resolved value overrides the constant in the config file
    Given a host that resolves a concurrency of 10
    And "extension/stryker.config.json" declares a concurrency of 1
    When the "mutation" entry point launches Stryker
    Then Stryker is invoked with concurrency 10

  # BL-786 mutation-concurrency-host-resolved-03
  Scenario Outline: Every mutation entry point resolves its own concurrency
    Given a host that resolves a concurrency of 10
    When the "<entry_point>" entry point launches Stryker
    Then Stryker is invoked with concurrency 10

    Examples:
      | entry_point                      |
      | mutation                         |
      | mutation:lets-talk-cursor-bridge |

  # BL-786 mutation-concurrency-host-resolved-04
  Scenario: No mutation entry point is left reading the frozen constant
    When the mutation entry points declared in "extension/package.json" are enumerated
    Then each mutation entry point resolves its concurrency through the shared resolver

  # BL-786 mutation-concurrency-host-resolved-05
  Scenario Outline: An explicit pin beats the computed value
    Given a host that resolves a concurrency of 10
    When the "mutation" entry point launches Stryker with concurrency pinned to <pin>
    Then Stryker is invoked with concurrency <pin>
    And the run output reports the concurrency as "pinned"

    Examples:
      | pin |
      | 1   |
      | 18  |

  # BL-786 mutation-concurrency-host-resolved-06
  Scenario: The run says which number it chose and what it was derived from
    Given a host with 20 cores and 10282 MB of free RAM
    When the "mutation" entry point launches Stryker
    Then the run output reports the resolved concurrency 10
    And the run output reports the free RAM, core count, per-worker peak and reserve it was derived from
    And the run output reports the concurrency as "computed"

  # BL-786 mutation-concurrency-host-resolved-07
  Scenario: The per-worker peak has one definition that every entry point follows
    Given the declared per-worker peak RSS is 1566 MB (1642242048 bytes)
    And a host with 20 cores and 10282 MB of free RAM
    When each mutation entry point resolves its concurrency
    Then each mutation entry point resolves a concurrency of 5
